{
  "unit": {
    "id": "4",
    "title": "Family and friends",
    "blurb": "my / your / our, people, and 3rd-declension nouns",
    "intro": "Possessives like _meus, -a, -um_ (my), _tuus_ (your) and _noster_ (our) are adjectives, so they agree with the noun in gender, number and case: _pater meus_ (my father), _māter mea_ (my mother), _frātrēs meī_ (my brothers). Some nouns follow a third pattern, the third declension: _pater, māter, mīles, rēx_ and _homō_ have different endings (_patrēs, mātrēs, mīlitēs_) which you will learn simply by meeting them. Adjectives such as _fortis_ (brave) and _tristis_ (sad) use the same endings for masculine and feminine."
  },
